1432],{},[18,1415,1416],{},"Idiom",": use ",[63,1419,600],{}," and watch ",[63,1422,636],{}," (not ",[63,1425,87],{},") — Linux uses free RAM for cache (good). ",[63,1428,636],{}," is what apps can actually use. Low ",[63,1431,636],{}," = memory pressure.",[417,1434,1435,1417,1437,1420,1439,1441,1442,1444,1445,1447],{},[18,1436,1416],{},[63,1438,1382],{},[63,1440,109],{}," + ",[63,1443,106],{}," — ",[63,1446,109],{}," near 100% = disk saturated. This is the #1 cause of \"the app is slow\" — usually disk, not CPU.",[417,1449,1450,1417,1452,1441,1455,1458,1459,1462],{},[18,1451,1416],{},[63,1453,1454],{},"nice -n 19",[63,1456,1457],{},"ionice -c3"," for non-urgent heavy jobs — backups, ",[63,1460,1461],{},"find \u002F",", indexing. Prevents heavy jobs from disrupting interactive work.",[417,1464,1465,1467,1468,1471],{},[18,1466,1416],{},": set ",[63,1469,1470],{},"vm.swappiness=10"," on servers (1 for databases) — default 60 swaps too eagerly for production.",[417,1473,1474,1476,1477,1480,1481,1484],{},[18,1475,1416],{},": use systemd ",[63,1478,1479],{},"MemoryMax="," to limit service memory — prevents a buggy service from eating all RAM and triggering OOM killer (which might kill the ",[587,1482,1483],{},"wrong"," process).",[417,1486,1487,1417,1490,1492,1493,1495,1496,1499,1500,1502],{},[18,1488,1489],{},"Debug",[63,1491,1353],{}," and check ",[63,1494,399],{}," (I\u002FO wait) + ",[63,1497,1498],{},"b"," (blocked) — if load is high but ",[63,1501,369],{}," (user CPU) is low, the bottleneck is disk I\u002FO, not CPU. This is the most common \"high load but CPU looks fine\" mystery.",[417,1504,1505,1417,1507,1510,1511,1513,1514,1517,1518,1513,1520,1523],{},[18,1506,1489],{},[63,1508,1509],{},"perf top"," to find the exact function using CPU — ",[63,1512,65],{}," shows which ",[587,1515,1516],{},"process"," is busy, ",[63,1519,1509],{},[587,1521,1522],{},"function"," within it.",[417,1525,1526,1417,1528,1531],{},[18,1527,1489],{},[63,1529,1530],{},"journalctl -k | grep -i oom"," after a mysterious process death — the OOM killer logs which process it killed and why.",[23,1533,1535],{"id":1534},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[414,1537,1538,1556,1574,1595,1608,1631,1644,1661,1670,1683],{},[417,1539,1540,1543,1544,1547,1548,1444,1550,1552,1553,1555],{},[18,1541,1542],{},"High load average isn't always CPU",": load includes processes in ",[63,1545,1546],{},"D"," state (uninterruptible I\u002FO wait). High load + low CPU = disk bottleneck. Check ",[63,1549,90],{},[63,1551,1498],{}," (blocked) and ",[63,1554,399],{}," (I\u002FO wait) reveal the true cause.",[417,1557,1558,1563,1564,1566,1567,1569,1570,1573],{},[18,1559,1560,1562],{},[63,1561,87],{}," being low is normal",": Linux uses free RAM for page cache. ",[63,1565,87],{}," near 0 is expected. Watch ",[63,1568,636],{}," — if ",[587,1571,1572],{},"that's"," low, you're out of memory.",[417,1575,1576,1579,1580,1357,1583,1586,1587,1589,1590,1357,1592,1594],{},[18,1577,1578],{},"Swapping kills performance silently",": even small ",[63,1581,1582],{},"si",[63,1584,1585],{},"so"," in ",[63,1588,90],{}," causes huge latency spikes (disk is ~1000x slower than RAM). If ",[63,1591,1582],{},[63,1593,1585],{}," is non-zero, you have memory pressure.",[417,1596,1597,1600,1601,1604,1605,644],{},[18,1598,1599],{},"OOM killer may kill the wrong process",": it picks the highest ",[63,1602,1603],{},"oom_score"," (biggest memory user) — which might be your database. Protect with ",[63,1606,1607],{},"OOMScoreAdjust=-1000",[417,1609,1610,1615,1616,1619,1620,1622,1623,1357,1625,1627,1628,1630],{},[18,1611,1612,1614],{},[63,1613,457],{}," doesn't affect I\u002FO or memory",": a ",[63,1617,1618],{},"nice 19"," process still hammers the disk. Use ",[63,1621,1457],{}," for I\u002FO and ",[63,1624,1225],{},[63,1626,1267],{}," for memory. ",[63,1629,457],{}," is CPU-only.",[417,1632,1633,1639,1640,1643],{},[18,1634,1635,1638],{},[63,1636,1637],{},"ionice -c1"," (realtime) can starve the disk",": it blocks all other I\u002FO. Never use on a shared system. Use ",[63,1641,1642],{},"-c3"," (idle).",[417,1645,1646,1652,1653,1656,1657,1660],{},[18,1647,1648,1651],{},[63,1649,1650],{},"noatime"," can break some apps",": a few apps (like ",[63,1654,1655],{},"mutt",") rely on access times. ",[63,1658,1659],{},"relatime"," (default) is safer.",[417,1662,1663,1669],{},[18,1664,1665,1668],{},[63,1666,1667],{},"barrier=0"," risks data corruption on power loss",": faster but risks filesystem corruption. Don't use in production unless you have a battery-backed cache.",[417,1671,1672,1675,1676,66,1679,1682],{},[18,1673,1674],{},"Tuning can make things worse",": changing ",[63,1677,1678],{},"tcp_tw_reuse",[63,1680,1681],{},"dirty_ratio",", scheduler without measuring can degrade performance. Always benchmark before and after. Keep changes small and test one at a time.",[417,1684,1685,1688],{},[18,1686,1687],{},"BBR can hurt on some networks",": great on high-latency\u002Flossy links but can be aggressive on local networks. Test before deploying.",[23,1690,1692],{"id":1691},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1694,1695,1696,1698],{},"A server is slow. The admin checks ",[63,1697,248],{},":",[165,1700,1703],{"className":1701,"code":1702,"language":957,"meta":169},[955],"load average: 16.20, 15.85, 15.70\n",[63,1704,1702],{"__ignoreMap":169},[14,1706,1707,1708,1710,1711,1713,1714,1716,1717,1719],{},"They check ",[63,1709,65],{}," — CPU usage is 20% (",[63,1712,369],{}," 15%, ",[63,1715,379],{}," 5%, ",[63,1718,389],{}," 80%). They conclude \"the load is wrong, CPU is mostly idle.\" They check CPU count: 4 cores. What's actually happening, and what should they check next?",[1721,1722,1723,1727,1739,1746,1751,1828,1831,1870],"details",{},[1724,1725,1726],"summary",{},"Answer",[14,1728,1729,1735,1736,1738],{},[18,1730,1731,1732,1734],{},"The load average includes processes in ",[63,1733,1546],{}," state (uninterruptible I\u002FO wait), not just CPU-bound processes."," Load 16 on a 4-core system with 80% idle CPU means ~14 processes are stuck in I\u002FO wait (state ",[63,1737,1546],{},"), not waiting for CPU.",[14,1740,1741,1742,1745],{},"The bottleneck is ",[18,1743,1744],{},"disk I\u002FO",", not CPU.",[14,1747,1748],{},[18,1749,1750],{},"Check:",[161,1752,1753],{"language":163},[165,1754,1756],{"className":167,"code":1755,"language":163,"meta":169,"style":169},"vmstat 1\n#  r  b  swpd  free  buff  cache  si  so  bi  bo  in  cs  us  sy  id  wa  st\n#  0  14     0 200M   1G    8G     0   0  40  80  ...  ...  15   5  20  60   0\n#                    ↑                                                ↑\n#  b=14 (14 processes in D state)                      wa=60 (60% I\u002FO wait)\n\niostat -x 1\n# Device  %util  await\n# sda      98     45.2\n# → disk is 98% utilized, 45ms average wait = saturated\n\niotop -o\n# → find which process is doing the I\u002FO\n",[63,1757,1758,1765,1770,1775,1780,1785,1789,1797,1802,1807,1812,1816,1823],{"__ignoreMap":169},[173,1759,1760,1762],{"class":175,"line":176},[173,1761,90],{"class":186},[173,1763,1764],{"class":258}," 1\n",[173,1766,1767],{"class":175,"line":183},[173,1768,1769],{"class":179},"#  r  b  swpd  free  buff  cache  si  so  bi  bo  in  cs  us  sy  id  wa  st\n",[173,1771,1772],{"class":175,"line":190},[173,1773,1774],{"class":179},"#  0  14     0 200M   1G    8G     0   0  40  80  ...  ...  15   5  20  60   0\n",[173,1776,1777],{"class":175,"line":196},[173,1778,1779],{"class":179},"#                    ↑                                                ↑\n",[173,1781,1782],{"class":175,"line":202},[173,1783,1784],{"class":179},"#  b=14 (14 processes in D state)                      wa=60 (60% I\u002FO wait)\n",[173,1786,1787],{"class":175,"line":209},[173,1788,206],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":205},[173,1790,1791,1793,1795],{"class":175,"line":218},[173,1792,115],{"class":186},[173,1794,1034],{"class":258},[173,1796,1764],{"class":258},[173,1798,1799],{"class":175,"line":224},[173,1800,1801],{"class":179},"# Device  %util  await\n",[173,1803,1804],{"class":175,"line":311},[173,1805,1806],{"class":179},"# sda      98     45.2\n",[173,1808,1809],{"class":175,"line":317},[173,1810,1811],{"class":179},"# → disk is 98% utilized, 45ms average wait = saturated\n",[173,1813,1814],{"class":175,"line":323},[173,1815,206],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":205},[173,1817,1818,1820],{"class":175,"line":329},[173,1819,118],{"class":186},[173,1821,1822],{"class":258}," -o\n",[173,1824,1825],{"class":175,"line":335},[173,1826,1827],{"class":179},"# → find which process is doing the I\u002FO\n",[14,1829,1830],{},"The fix depends on the cause:",[414,1832,1833,1839,1851,1864],{},[417,1834,1835,1838],{},[18,1836,1837],{},"Disk too slow",": upgrade to SSD, add more disks (RAID), or use a faster filesystem.",[417,1840,1841,1844,1845,1847,1848,1850],{},[18,1842,1843],{},"One process hogging I\u002FO",": ",[63,1846,1457],{}," the offender, or use ",[63,1849,1225],{}," to limit I\u002FO.",[417,1852,1853,1856,1857,1859,1860,1863],{},[18,1854,1855],{},"Memory pressure causing swap I\u002FO",": check ",[63,1858,600],{}," and ",[63,1861,1862],{},"vmstat si\u002Fso"," — add RAM or reduce memory usage.",[417,1865,1866,1869],{},[18,1867,1868],{},"Too many concurrent I\u002FO operations",": tune the application to batch writes or use async I\u002FO.",[14,1871,1872,1873],{},"The key insight: ",[18,1874,1875],{},"load average is not a CPU metric — it's a \"processes waiting for resources\" metric. High load + low CPU = I\u002FO bottleneck.",[1877,1878,1879],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .sdCPZ, html code.shiki .sdCPZ{--shiki-default:#6A737D;--shiki-github-dark:#6A737D}html pre.shiki code .sIsaT, html code.shiki .sIsaT{--shiki-default:#6F42C1;--shiki-github-dark:#B392F0}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html.github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html pre.shiki code .snvgF, html code.shiki .snvgF{--shiki-default:#005CC5;--shiki-github-dark:#79B8FF}html pre.shiki code .svdQ7, html code.shiki .svdQ7{--shiki-default:#D73A49;--shiki-github-dark:#F97583}html pre.shiki code .sJ6F3, html code.shiki .sJ6F3{--shiki-default:#032F62;--shiki-github-dark:#9ECBFF}html pre.shiki code .ssxIu, html code.shiki .ssxIu{--shiki-default:#24292E;--shiki-github-dark:#E1E4E8}",{"title":169,"searchDepth":183,"depth":183,"links":1881},[1882,1883,1892,1901,1907,1911,1913,1914,1915,1916],{"id":25,"depth":183,"text":26},{"id":154,"depth":183,"text":56,"children":1884},[1885,1886,1887,1889,1890],{"id":158,"depth":190,"text":159},{"id":230,"depth":190,"text":231},{"id":341,"depth":190,"text":1888},"CPU States (from vmstat \u002F mpstat)",{"id":439,"depth":190,"text":440},{"id":497,"depth":190,"text":1891},"perf — Profiling",{"id":593,"depth":183,"text":79,"children":1893},[1894,1896,1898,1899,1900],{"id":596,"depth":190,"text":1895},"Understanding free -h",{"id":655,"depth":190,"text":1897},"Edge Case: Low free Is Normal",{"id":689,"depth":190,"text":690},{"id":775,"depth":190,"text":776},{"id":896,"depth":190,"text":896},{"id":944,"depth":183,"text":100,"children":1902},[1903,1905],{"id":947,"depth":190,"text":1904},"iostat -x Key Columns",{"id":1068,"depth":190,"text":1906},"I\u002FO Priority: ionice",{"id":1150,"depth":183,"text":128,"children":1908},[1909,1910],{"id":1153,"depth":190,"text":1154},{"id":1163,"depth":190,"text":1164},{"id":1222,"depth":183,"text":1912},"cgroups — Resource Limits",{"id":1337,"depth":183,"text":1338},{"id":1408,"depth":183,"text":1409},{"id":1534,"depth":183,"text":1535},{"id":1691,"depth":183,"text":1692},"Linux performance tuning is about finding the bottleneck (CPU, memory, disk I\u002FO, network) and addressing it — without making things worse. This chapter covers the tools to identify bottlenecks, the tuning knobs, and the kernel's built-in responses (OOM killer, swappiness).","md",{},"\u002Flinux\u002F15-performance-and-tuning",{"title":5,"description":1917},"linux\u002F15-performance-and-tuning","6oDPVjEI9A69o15k4YzzW_zdah81Ac9a3VetrUIioQ4",1789140923643]